A||||3||72vinman||Plenum paint refinish. HELP.||||5||
Z||000000||72vinman||09-10-2001||08:47 PM||||I have been through the archives but have only found info on repainting the Plenum. The problem I have is that the paint has chipped away in many areas revealing the bare aluminum. Do I have to sand the entire plenum down to the aluminum and prime it before I paint it. Any tips would really really help. Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||09-10-2001||09:07 PM||||I was told when I got some paint for my plenum that priming wasn't necessary.  The primer would likely not stand up to the heat anyway.  But get some good chemical stripper and strip the old paint off, or sand if you prefer.  Just be sure to clean any chemicals off very thoroughly.  Then paint with your choice of High Temp Engine paint.  I'm also checking into powder coating.  The Fiero Store has recently started offering that, but for $175 for plenum and valve covers, I think I'll see about doing it myself.  Besides, I don't want to send them my engraved plenum as a core for a non-engraved one (those are the only ones they're offering right now).||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||09-10-2001||09:48 PM||||In the cases where you have any bare aluminium, you must get a metal prep for the aluminium, sets up a chemical conversion coating for paint adhesion. You can get it a all Automotive Painters Supply Shops.<P>If you don't take this important step, you stand the risk of the paint peeling down the road.||||reg||1
Z||000003||72vinman||09-10-2001||09:55 PM||||Thanks for the tips guys.<BR>California, I think the previous owner did not use that metal prep when he painted becuase the paint is peeling off.||||reg||1
